timestamp=2026-11-04T15:46:07.240Z level=INFO run=7dbbc1d1 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="cat >> /app/compute_salaries.py << 'PY'\n\ndef get_billing_month_key(dt, start_weekday):\n    \"\"\"\n    For each month, the billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week\n    inherited from the dataset's earliest record (Tuesday, start_weekday=1).\n    We need to assign each work record to a billing month. \n    Common convention: the billing month is determined by the billing period it belongs to.\n    Or alternatively, group by the month that contains the billing period start?\n    The question says \"total monthly salary for each employee for every month\" - so results should be per month.\n    And \"On that day, employees are required to appear at the workplace to collect their salary.\"\n    This suggests billing periods are month-long periods that all start on that weekday (e.g., if start is Tuesday, \n    each billing period starts on the first/defined Tuesday of/relative to month, or simply periods aligned to that weekday).\n    But how exactly? Another interpretation: the billing month is defined such that its billing period starts on that day of week.\n    Or maybe the billing month is identified by (year, month) but the cutoff is that weekday? \n    Or perhaps the \"billing month\" is the calendar month, but the pay day is fixed to that weekday? That doesn't change which month a work record belongs to.\n    Or maybe the billing period spans across month boundaries and we should group by the billing period's anchor month \n    (e.g., the month when that period's start falls, or when salary is collected on that weekday).\n    Let us look at the data patterns - are there multiple records per month? And what are the date ranges?\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
