timestamp=2026-11-04T15:46:42.354Z level=INFO run=7dbbc1d1 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Data spans March to June 2025. So 4 months total. The billing period starts on Tuesday (from earliest record) for all employees each month.\n# That means each billing period is a month-long period starting on Tuesday? Or starting on the Tuesday of that month?\n# For example, earliest is 2025-03-04 which is a Tuesday. So for March, billing period starts on 2025-03-04 (Tuesday). \n# Then subsequent billing periods would start on the same weekday each month? Or every 4 weeks? Or on the first Tuesday of each month? Or simply aligned such that start day is Tuesday - maybe billing periods are defined as starting on Tuesdays and lasting until next Tuesday (weekly) or monthly periods anchored to that Tuesday?\n# The task says \"total monthly salary for each employee for every month\" and \"billing period for all employees starts on the same day of the week inherited from the dataset's earliest record\". This phrasing is a bit ambiguous, but most natural interpretation: each month has a billing period that starts on that specific weekday (Tuesday). Also \"On that day, employees are required to appear at the workplace to collect their salary.\" - payday happens on that Tuesday each month? \n# But months have different lengths. Alternatively, billing periods are calendar months but payday is that weekday - that doesn’t affect how we group work records. Or billing periods are 4-week periods anchored to that Tuesday. Let us look at how work is distributed across dates relative to 2025-03-04 being Tuesday.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
